course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 5
title: How old are you, where from, and meet the family
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In Ukrainian you do not say «I am nine» — the years are «to you»: SKILky tobI rokIv? is «how many years to you?», and the answer is meni DEvyat rokIv, «to me nine years».
explain: Kids also ask zvidky ty? (where are you from?), de ty zhyvEsh? (where do you live?) and ty z YakOho klAsu? (which class are you in?).
explain: Then Sashko brings you into the kitchen: tse miy druh — this is my friend — and you meet MAma, TAto, BAbusya and diDUS.
explain: Watch the little words: druh is a friend who is a boy, podRUha a friend who is a girl, and neither means a boyfriend or girlfriend among kids.
fact: SKILky tobI rokIv? means how old are you?, literally «how many years to you?».
fact: meni DEvyat rokIv means I am nine years old; rokIv (years) follows 5 and up, while after 2 to 4 it is roky.
fact: meni DEsyat means I'm ten; DEsyat only sounds a bit like «death-yacht» — it is just the number ten.
fact: de ty zhyvEsh? means where do you live?, and an answer is ya zhyvU tut, I live here.
fact: zvidky ty? means where are you from?, and an answer is ya z AnHliyi, I'm from England.
fact: ty z YakOho klAsu? means which class are you in?; klAs is the year group, and an answer is ya v chetvErtomu, in fourth.
fact: tse miy druh means this is my friend, for a boy; druh has nothing to do with English «drug».
fact: tse moya podRUha means this is my friend, for a girl; among kids podRUha does not mean a girlfriend.
fact: MAma means mum; jokes about someone's mum can upset people, so they are best avoided.
fact: TAto means dad; some families say pApa, but TAto is the most Ukrainian word.
fact: BAbusya is a loving word for granny; BAba alone about a strange woman can sound rude.
fact: diDUS is a loving word for grandad, DId is shorter, and DId MorOz is Grandfather Frost, an old New Year figure.
exercise: What does SKILky tobI rokIv? mean? | How old are you? | How many sweets have you got? | What time is it? | How tall are you?
exercise: What is the word-for-word meaning of SKILky tobI rokIv? | How many years to you? | How many years ago? | How many rocks do you have? | How many friends do you have?
exercise: You are nine. What do you say? | meni DEvyat rokIv | meni DEsyat | ya DEvyat | DEvyat tobI
exercise: What does meni DEsyat mean? | I'm ten | I'm tired | I'm sixteen | I'm from Desyat
exercise: What does de ty zhyvEsh? mean? | Where do you live? | Where are you going? | Who do you live with? | Are you alive and well?
exercise: What does zvidky ty? mean? | Where are you from? | When are you leaving? | Who are you? | Why are you here?
exercise: How can you answer zvidky ty? | ya z AnHliyi | ya zhyvU tut | meni DEsyat | tse miy druh
exercise: What is ty z YakOho klAsu? asking? | Which school year you are in | What your favourite lesson is | Which classroom is yours | How clever you are
exercise: Sashko points to you and says tse miy druh. What is he saying? | This is my friend | This is my brother | This is my drug | This is my neighbour
exercise: What does tse moya podRUha mean among kids? | This is my friend (a girl) | This is my girlfriend, we're dating | This is my sister | This is my teacher
exercise: What do Ukrainian kids call their mum? | MAma | TAtko | BAbusya | TItka
exercise: Which word for dad is the most Ukrainian? | TAto | pApa | DId | brat
exercise: Why is BAbusya better than BAba for the friend's granny? | BAbusya is loving, BAba alone can sound rude | BAba means grandad | BAbusya means great-granny | BAba is only for cats
exercise: What is diDUS? | A loving word for grandad | A word for an uncle or older cousin | A small dog | A New Year tree
exercise: Who is DId MorOz? | Grandfather Frost, an old New Year figure | Sashko's real grandad, who lives in the village | A football player | A kind of ice cream
exercise: Why do people avoid «your mum» jokes in Ukrainian? | They can upset people | MAma is a rude word | Mums are not allowed in jokes by law | Nobody understands them
test: «podruha means girlfriend so Sashko's in loooove» Which is right? | No, among kids podRUha just means a friend who is a girl | Yes, it always means girlfriend | Yes, and druh means boyfriend | No, podRUha means sister
test: «druh = drug, that's dodgy» What is right? | No, druh just means friend, a boy | Yes, it means medicine from the chemist | Yes, it is a bad word | No, druh means dragon
test: A kid asks «SKILky tobI rokIv?» and you're 10. What fits? | meni DEsyat | meni DEvyat rokIv | ya z AnHliyi | ya v chetvErtomu
test: «you say ya DEsyat for I'm ten, like English» Which is right? | No: meni DEsyat, «to me ten» | Yes, ya DEsyat is right | No: DEsyat ya tobI | No: ya rokIv DEsyat
test: What would happen if you called the friend's granny «BAba» to her face as a stranger would? | It could sound a bit rude; BAbusya is loving | Nothing, BAba is the loving word | She would think you meant grandad | She would think you said «father»
test: A new kid asks «de ty zhyvEsh?». What is he asking? | Where you live | Where you were born | Where your dad works | Where you are going now
test: «zhyvesh, zhyvy… is he asking if I'm alive lol» Which is the best answer? | zhyv- is about living; here it asks where you live | Yes, he's checking you're alive and breathing | No, it's about food | No, it's about a dog that lives in your street and barks
test: Sashko introduces his mum. Before you say DObryi DEN, what does he probably say? | tse moya MAma | tse moya podRUha | tse miy druh | tse moya BAba
test: In a story a boy says «tse miy druh» about a girl. What should it be? | tse moya podRUha | tse miy druha | tse moya druh | tse miy podRUh
test: «what's DId MorOz, Sashko's grandad?» Which is right? | No, Grandfather Frost, an old New Year figure | Yes, it's his grandad's name | It's a frozen dessert | It's a winter football club
test: «my ten-year-old cousin is in fourth» says Sashko. Which question would he have answered? | ty z YakOho klAsu? | zvidky ty? | de ty zhyvEsh zaraz? | yak teBE zvut?
exam: Ascertain the literal construction of the enquiry SKILky tobI rokIv? | How many years are there to you? | How many years have you lived here? | How many years until your birthday? | How many years have you attended school?
exam: Identify the expression by which a boy introduces a female companion without any romantic implication. | tse moya podRUha | tse miy druh | tse moya MAma | tse moya BAbusya
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of BAbusya and BAba. | BAbusya is affectionate, whereas BAba alone, said of an unfamiliar woman, may appear discourteous | Both are equally formal titles | BAba is the more affectionate form | BAbusya signifies grandfather
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the enquiry zvidky ty? | It requests the place from which the person originates | It requests the person's present street address and house number | It requests the person's age | It requests the person's school year
